export default {"id":"sounddesign-level-10","number":10,"title":"Vital","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Master the modern visual sound design architecture of Vital (spectral wavetable synthesizer). - Navigate the primary interface sections: 3 Wavetable Oscillators, Sample/Noise Generator, Multi-Mode Filter, Envelopes (1–3), LFOs (1–4), Modu","modules":[{"id":"sounddesign-m-10-1-1","code":"10.1","title":"Vital Interface & Wavetable Engine","blurb":"- Master the modern visual sound design architecture of Vital (spectral wavetable synthesizer). - Navigate the primary interface sections: 3 Wavetable Oscillators, Sample/Noise Generator, Multi-Mode Filter, Envelopes (1–3), LFOs (1–4), Modu","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-36","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Master the modern visual sound design architecture of <strong>Vital</strong> (spectral wavetable synthesizer).","Navigate the primary interface sections: <strong>3 Wavetable Oscillators</strong>, <strong>Sample/Noise Generator</strong>, <strong>Multi-Mode Filter</strong>, <strong>Envelopes (1–3)</strong>, <strong>LFOs (1–4)</strong>, <strong>Modulation Matrix Tab</strong>, and <strong>Effects Tab</strong>.","Define the physical structure of a digital <strong>wavetable</strong> as an indexed array of up to 256 single-cycle waveform frames.","Control <strong>Wavetable Position</strong> and calibrate <strong>Frame Interpolation Modes</strong> (None, Spectral Morph, Waveform Linear).","Interpret the real-time <strong>3D Wavetable Terrain Visualizer</strong>.","Operate the integrated <strong>Vital Wavetable Editor</strong> and deploy the <strong>Sample / Noise Oscillator</strong> for transient layering."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-1","title":"10.1.1 — What Vital Is","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.1 — What Vital Is","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Vital</strong> is a visual spectral wavetable synthesizer. It combines high-resolution wavetable frame scanning with frequency-domain spectral warping, drag-and-drop modulation routing, and per-voice polyphonic unison processing."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                       VITAL AT A GLANCE                                           |\n+---------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Oscillators**     | 3 independent Wavetable Oscillators + 1 dedicated Sample / Noise Generator. |\n+---------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Spectral Warps**  | Frequency-domain manipulation (Harmonic Stretch, Formant, Smear, Vocode).   |\n+---------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Visual Matrix**   | Instant drag-and-drop modulation with continuous real-time visual feedback. |\n+---------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Unison Engine**   | Up to 16 detuned unison voices per oscillator with stereo spread &amp; blend.   |\n+---------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Effects Section** | 9 re-orderable internal effect modules with polyphonic modulation access.   |\n+---------------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Vital delivers visual clarity where every parameter change is rendered graphically in real time at 60 FPS."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 1 (Overview &amp; Architecture)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §7"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-2","title":"10.1.2 — Vital Panel Tour","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.2 — Vital Panel Tour","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Vital interface is partitioned into logical functional tiers:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                         VITAL GUI MAP                                             |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [ HAMBURGER MENU / INIT ]  | [ PRESET BROWSER ]   | [ MACROS 1 - 4 ]   | [ MASTER VOLUME &amp; OSC SCOPE ]|\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| TOP SECTION: OSCILLATORS (OSC 1, OSC 2, OSC 3) &amp; SAMPLE / NOISE GENERATOR                         |\n| - 3D Wavetable Display    - Pitch, Transpose, Tune  - Unison Voices &amp; Detune                      |\n| - Wavetable Position Knob - Spectral Warp Mode/Knob - Filter 1/2 Routing &amp; Direct Out Toggles     |\n+---------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| MIDDLE SECTION: DUAL FILTERS                      | MODULATORS: ENVELOPES &amp; LFOs                  |\n| - Filter 1 &amp; Filter 2 (Analog, Clean, Comb, Form) | - Envelope 1 (Amp), Envelope 2, Envelope 3    |\n| - Cutoff, Res, Drive, Blend, Keytrack, Stereo Pan | - LFO 1, LFO 2, LFO 3, LFO 4 (Bezier MSEG)    |\n+---------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------------------------+\n| HEADER NAVIGATION: [ VOICE TAB ]                  | [ EFFECTS TAB ]        | [ MATRIX TAB ]       |\n+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 1.1 (GUI Navigation)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-3","title":"10.1.3 — What a Wavetable Actually Is","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.3 — What a Wavetable Actually Is","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"While a traditional virtual analog oscillator loops a single geometric cycle (such as one static sawtooth wave), a <strong>Wavetable</strong> is an indexed array of multiple discrete single-cycle waveforms called <strong>Frames</strong> (typically 64 to 256 individual frames stacked sequentially)."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Wavetable Structure (256 Single-Cycle Frames):\n[ Frame 1: Pure Sine ] ===&gt; [ Frame 64: Triangle ] ===&gt; [ Frame 128: Sawtooth ] ===&gt; [ Frame 256: Jagged Metal ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Frame Progression:\nFrame 1:    /\\        (Pure Sine Fundamental)\n           /  \\\nFrame 128:  /|  /|    (Sawtooth: Full Harmonic Spectrum)\n           / | / |\nFrame 256: /\\/\\/\\/\\   (High-Order Inharmonic Metal)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"By reading through the table at an audio-rate cycle (440 Hz), the synth generates pitch. By shifting which <em>frame</em> is currently being read, the synth alters its fundamental harmonic spectrum."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2 (Wavetable Mechanics)","<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 9.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-4","title":"10.1.4 — Wavetable Position: Dynamic Scanning","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.4 — Wavetable Position: Dynamic Scanning","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Wave Frame (Wavetable Position)</strong> knob controls which frame in the array is currently being played:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ LFO 1 (1-Bar Synced Ramp) ] ===(Drag-and-Drop)===&gt; [ OSC 1 WAVETABLE POSITION ]\n                                                                |\n                                                                v\n                                               [ Scans from Frame 1 to Frame 256 ]\n                                                                |\n                                                                v\n                                              [ Smoothly Morphs Timbre Over Time ]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["When modulated with an LFO or Envelope, the oscillator continuously morphs from smooth, rounded waveforms into jagged, aggressive overtones and back."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.1 (Wave Frame Modulation)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-5","title":"10.1.5 — Frame Interpolation Modes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.5 — Frame Interpolation Modes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When the Wavetable Position rests between two discrete frames (e.g., Frame 12.4), Vital calculates the intermediate waveform using <strong>Frame Interpolation</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Interpolation Mode   | Mathematical Algorithm &amp; Acoustic Result                       |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **None (Off)**       | Stepped jumps between frames. Produces gritty, 8-bit digital   |\n|                      | stepping and chiptune zipper noise as position moves.          |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Waveform (Linear)**| Time-domain linear crossfade between adjacent frames. Smooth   |\n|                      | and natural for standard analog waveform transitions.          |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Spectral Morph**   | Frequency-domain FFT interpolation. Analyzes individual partial|\n|                      | amplitudes and phases, morphing harmonics with zero phase drop.|\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Standard Rule:</strong> Use <strong>Spectral Morph</strong> for complex vocal formants and digital pads to eliminate phase cancellations during morphing."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.2 (Interpolation Topologies)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-6","title":"10.1.6 — The 3D Wavetable Visualizer","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.6 — The 3D Wavetable Visualizer","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital features an interactive 3D terrain visualizer inside each oscillator block:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ 3D TERRAIN DISPLAY ]\n  - Horizontal X-Axis: Waveform Phase Cycle (0 to 360 degrees)\n  - Vertical Y-Axis:   Waveform Amplitude (+1.0 to -1.0)\n  - Depth Z-Axis:      Wavetable Frame Index (Frame 1 to 256)\n  - Orange Playhead:   Current Active Interpolated Frame</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Clicking the display toggles between <strong>3D Perspective View</strong>, <strong>2D Waveform Cross-Section</strong>, and <strong>2D Frequency Spectrum Bins</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-7","title":"10.1.7 — The Vital Wavetable Editor","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.7 — The Vital Wavetable Editor","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Clicking the <strong>Pencil Icon</strong> above the 3D visualizer opens the full-screen <strong>Wavetable Editor</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         VITAL WAVETABLE EDITOR MODES                          |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Wave Source**      | Draw single-cycle curves using Bezier vector points.   |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Audio Splicer**    | Drag in any `.wav` sample; Vital automatically slices  |\n|                      | it into pitch-detected single-cycle frames.            |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Spectral Morph**   | Draw harmonic partial amplitudes directly across 256   |\n|                      | frequency bins (Additive wavetable design).            |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Text to Wavetable**| Type any text string; Vital's speech synthesizer       |\n|                      | generates a custom formant-spliced speech wavetable.   |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 3 (Wavetable Editor &amp; Splicer)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-8","title":"10.1.8 — Initializing Vital","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.8 — Initializing Vital","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To establish a clean sound design starting point in Vital:"},{"type":"list","items":["Click the <strong>Hamburger Menu icon ()</strong> in the top title bar.","Select <strong><code>Initialize Preset</code></strong>.","Verify: Osc 1 is set to an initialized <strong>Sawtooth wave</strong>, Filter is <strong>Bypassed</strong>, all Effects are <strong>Disabled</strong>, and all Matrix modulations are <strong>Cleared</strong>."],"ordered":true},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 1.3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-1-9","title":"10.1.9 — The Sample / Noise Oscillator","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.1.9 — The Sample / Noise Oscillator","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital includes a dedicated 4th generator: the <strong>Sample / Noise Oscillator</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ SAMPLE / NOISE GENERATOR ]\n  - Sample Selection: Factory Noise (White, Pink, Crackle), Key Clicks, Vinyl, Attack Transients\n  - Keytracking Toggle: Locks noise pitch to keyboard notes (Pitched Noise)\n  - Loop Modes: One-Shot (Transients) vs. Continuous Looping (Atmospheric Hiss)\n  - Filter Routing: Route directly to Filter 1, Filter 2, or Direct Out</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Application:</em> Layering a 15 ms wooden acoustic mallet click over an initialized synth pluck."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.4 (Sample Oscillator)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-10-2-2","code":"10.2","title":"Spectral Warping in Vital","blurb":"- Define the operational architecture of Spectral Warping: mathematical manipulation of frequency bins in the spectral domain prior to the subtractive filter stage. - Differentiate and operate Frequency-Domain Spectral Warps (Formant Scale,","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-37","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Define the operational architecture of <strong>Spectral Warping</strong>: mathematical manipulation of frequency bins in the spectral domain <em>prior to</em> the subtractive filter stage.","Differentiate and operate <strong>Frequency-Domain Spectral Warps</strong> (<strong>Formant Scale</strong>, <strong>Harmonic Stretch</strong>, <strong>Inharmonic Smear</strong>, <strong>Vocode</strong>) and <strong>Time-Domain Phase Warps</strong> (<strong>Sync</strong>, <strong>PWM</strong>, <strong>Bend ±</strong>, <strong>Squeeze</strong>).","Deploy <strong>Audio-Rate Cross-Oscillator FM / RM / AM</strong> inside Vital.","Construct vocal, talking dubstep growl timbres via real-time <strong>Formant Scale</strong> automation.","Formulate the fundamental distinction: <strong>Spectral Warping generates and shifts harmonic energy at the source; Subtractive Filtering attenuates existing energy downstream.</strong>"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-2-1","title":"10.2.1 — What Spectral Warping Is","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.1 — What Spectral Warping Is","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In traditional synthesizers, once an oscillator selects its waveform, that waveform's harmonic series is fixed until it reaches a filter."},{"type":"prose","html":"Vital introduces a dedicated <strong>Spectral Warp Engine</strong> directly inside the oscillator core. Before the waveform is rendered to audio or sent to the filter, its mathematical frequency spectrum is transformed via discrete DSP algorithms:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ RAW WAVETABLE FRAME ] ===&gt; [ SPECTRAL WARP ENGINE ] ===&gt; [ TRANSFORMED SPECTRUM ] ===&gt; [ FILTER MATRIX ]\n                                      ^\n                                      |\n                           [ Warp Amount &amp; Mode Knob ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Spectral warping enables you to bend phase, stretch harmonics, shift vocal formants, and perform audio-rate cross-modulation <em>at the generator stage</em>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.5 (Spectral Morph Modes)","<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 9.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-2-2","title":"10.2.2 — Frequency-Domain Spectral Warp Modes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.2 — Frequency-Domain Spectral Warp Modes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Spectral Warp Mode   | Mathematical Transformation &amp; Acoustic Result                  |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Formant Scale**    | Compresses or expands the entire harmonic spectrum while       |\n|                      | preserving fundamental pitch. Imparts human vocal tract formants|\n|                      | ($A, E, I, O, U$) and robotic vowels to any raw waveform.      |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Harmonic Stretch** | Compresses harmonics toward the fundamental or stretches them  |\n|                      | into upper registers. Generates tearing, metallic neuro grit.  |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Inharmonic Smear** | Blurs and de-correlates harmonic phase relationships. Converts |\n|                      | bright sawtooths into metallic glass, gong chimes, and noise.  |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Vocode**           | Convolves the waveform through a simulated internal 32-band    |\n|                      | vocoder filter bank for robotic speech textures.               |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Pitch Shift**      | Shifts the upper harmonic overtones independently while leaving|\n|                      | the fundamental bass root locked in place.                     |\n+----------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.5.1 (Spectral Domain Modes)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-2-3","title":"10.2.3 — Time-Domain Phase Warp Modes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.3 — Time-Domain Phase Warp Modes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In addition to frequency-domain processing, the warp engine provides time-domain geometric phase transformations:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Phase Warp Mode  | Geometric Phase Transformation                                     |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Sync**         | Windowed Hard Sync: Squeezes multiple waveform cycles into a       |\n|                  | single period. Sweeping warp amount produces screaming sync leads.|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **PWM**          | Pulse Width Modulation applied to ANY waveform (not just square).  |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Bend $\\pm$**   | Pinches the waveform phase toward the center or edges. Adds hollow |\n|                  | woody weight and vocal-like pinch.                                 |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Squeeze**      | Compresses the entire waveform into the first half of the cycle    |\n|                  | and inserts silence in the second half, multiplying harmonics.     |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.5.2 (Phase Morph Modes)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-2-4","title":"10.2.4 — Cross-Oscillator FM in Vital","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.4 — Cross-Oscillator FM in Vital","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital allows any oscillator to act as a <strong>Phase Modulation (FM)</strong> carrier modulated by another source:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                         CROSS-OSCILLATOR FM ROUTING                           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FM from Osc 2**    | Osc 1 phase is modulated by Osc 2 audio output.        |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FM from Osc 3**    | Osc 1 phase is modulated by Osc 3 audio output.        |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FM from Sample**   | Osc 1 phase is modulated by the Sample/Noise generator |\n|                      | (Produces chaotic metallic grit and textured FM growls)|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ OSC 2 (Muted / Modulator: Sine Wave) ] ===(Audio-Rate PM)===&gt; [ OSC 1 (Carrier) ] ===&gt; Audio Out\n                                                                        ^\n                                                                        |\n                                                          [ Warp Knob = FM Amount ]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["Mute Oscillator 2 in the mixer so its raw audio is not heard.","Set Oscillator 1 Warp Mode to <strong><code>FM from Osc 2</code></strong>.","Turning the Warp knob now acts as the <strong>Modulation Index (FM Amount)</strong> slider."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.5.3 (Cross-Modulation FM)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-2-5","title":"10.2.5 — RM and AM in Vital","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.5 — RM and AM in Vital","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>RM (Ring Modulation):</strong> Multiplies the instantaneous amplitude of Osc 1 by Osc 2 (A_out = Osc 1 × Osc 2), outputting sum and difference frequencies for bell-like clangor.","<strong>AM (Amplitude Modulation):</strong> Modulates the volume level of Osc 1 at audio rate via Osc 2, generating sidebands while retaining the original Carrier fundamental."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.5.3"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-2-6","title":"10.2.6 — Formant Scale: Talking Dubstep Growls","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.6 — Formant Scale: Talking Dubstep Growls","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To design the signature modern electronic \"talking growl\" in Vital:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ BASIC SAWTOOTH WAVETABLE ] ===&gt; [ SPECTRAL WARP: Formant Scale ] ===&gt; [ LOW-PASS FILTER ]\n                                                ^\n                                                |\n                                    [ LFO 1 (1/4th Note Sync) ]\n                                    (Sweeps Warp Knob from 20% to 85%)</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Acoustic Behavior:","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"As LFO 1 sweeps the <strong>Formant Scale</strong> knob across its range, the spectral formant peaks slide across harmonic bins, causing the synthesizer to vocalize clear, guttural \"Yoi-Yoi\", \"Wah-Wah\", and \"Ow-Ow\" vocal expressions without using a dedicated vowel filter."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Dubstep-Production-Beginner-Live12-Launchkey.md</em>, §6.2","<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-2-7","title":"10.2.7 — Harmonic Stretch: Modern Bass Textures","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.7 — Harmonic Stretch: Modern Bass Textures","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Setting Warp Mode to <strong><code>Harmonic Stretch</code></strong> and modulating it with an aggressive exponential decay envelope forces the upper harmonics to compress and expand rapidly across note onset."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Acoustic Result:</strong> Produces the characteristic metallic \"laser crunch\" and high-tech neurofunk texture used in modern drum &amp; bass and mid-tempo basslines."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Drum-and-Bass-Production-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §6.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-2-8","title":"10.2.8 — Spectral Warp vs. Subtractive Filtering","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.2.8 — Spectral Warp vs. Subtractive Filtering","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A sound designer must understand the fundamental physical boundary between these two stages:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n|       SPECTRAL WARP STAGE          |      SUBTRACTIVE FILTER STAGE      |\n|     (Frequency-Domain Creation)    |     (Frequency-Domain Removal)     |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+\n| - Operates **inside the oscillator**| - Operates **after the oscillator**|\n| - **CREATES &amp; TRANSPOSES** partials| - **ATTENUATES &amp; SHAPES** partials |\n|   (Can generate new high harmonics |   (Can only remove or boost energy |\n|   from a pure sine wave source)    |   already present in the signal)   |\n| - Mathematical bin shifting        | - Analog-modeled frequency slopes  |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"You use <strong>Spectral Warping</strong> to invent the raw vocal/metallic DNA of the sound; you use the <strong>Subtractive Filter</strong> to carve its final place in the mix."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 9.3","<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2 &amp; 4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-10-3-3","code":"10.3","title":"Filters, Voices & Unison in Vital","blurb":"- Master Vital's Dual Multi-Mode Filter Section and configure all routing permutations (per-oscillator routing, serial cascading). - Deploy specialized filter topologies: Analog (12/24 dB), Ladder, Dirty (Saturated), Formant, Comb, and Phas","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-38","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Master Vital's <strong>Dual Multi-Mode Filter Section</strong> and configure all routing permutations (per-oscillator routing, serial cascading).","Deploy specialized filter topologies: <strong>Analog (12/24 dB)</strong>, <strong>Ladder</strong>, <strong>Dirty (Saturated)</strong>, <strong>Formant</strong>, <strong>Comb</strong>, and <strong>Phase Disperser</strong>.","Operate the <strong>16-Voice Unison Engine</strong> and calibrate <strong>Detune</strong>, <strong>Blend</strong>, and <strong>Stereo Spread</strong>.","Synthesize the definitive <strong>16-Voice Euphoric Supersaw</strong> from an initialized state.","Configure the <strong>Voicing Panel</strong>: Polyphony (1-32), Mono, Legato, Portamento Glide Time, and Slope.","Deploy the <strong>Direct Out</strong> routing switch to isolate clean mono sub-basses from downstream filters and distortion effects."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-3-1","title":"10.3.1 — Vital Dual Filter Section","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.1 — Vital Dual Filter Section","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital provides two independent multi-mode filter modules: <strong>Filter 1</strong> and <strong>Filter 2</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                      VITAL FILTER SECTION                                         |\n+---------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n| FILTER 1            | FILTER 2            | SOURCE ROUTING TOGGLES                                |\n+---------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+\n| - Model Selector    | - Model Selector    | - **Osc 1:** Toggle routes Osc 1 to Filter 1 / 2.    |\n| - Filter Style (LP) | - Filter Style (LP) | - **Osc 2:** Toggle routes Osc 2 to Filter 1 / 2.    |\n| - Cutoff &amp; Res      | - Cutoff &amp; Res      | - **Osc 3:** Toggle routes Osc 3 to Filter 1 / 2.    |\n| - Drive &amp; Keytrack  | - Drive &amp; Keytrack  | - **Sample:** Toggle routes Noise to Filter 1 / 2.   |\n| - Filter Blend      | - Filter Blend      | - **Filter 1 $\\rightarrow$ Filter 2:** Cascades F1 into F2 (Serial).|\n+---------------------+---------------------+-------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Each filter contains a <strong>Morph / Style Blend</strong> slider that continuously sweeps the filter topology: Low-Pass (0%) Band-Pass (50%) High-Pass (100%)"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 4 (Filter Section)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-3-2","title":"10.3.2 — Filter Models in Vital","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.2 — Filter Models in Vital","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Filter Model     | Acoustic Character &amp; Production Role                               |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Analog**       | Modeled 12 dB and 24 dB analog circuits with warm resonance bite.  |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Ladder**       | Vintage 4-pole transistor ladder filter with rich low-end drive.   |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Dirty**        | Non-linear saturated filter circuit; drives aggressively into grit.|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Formant**      | Dual resonant vowel formant peaks ($A, E, I, O, U$).               |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Comb**         | Micro-delay feedback line for metallic resonances and flanging.    |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Phase**        | Multi-stage all-pass phase disperser for laser zaps and scoops.    |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 4.1 (Filter Model Profiles)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-3-3","title":"10.3.3 — Filter Routing Permutations","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.3 — Filter Routing Permutations","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital allows total flexibility over how sound generators route into the filters:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">PARALLEL FILTERING:\n[ OSC 1 &amp; OSC 2 ] ===&gt; [ FILTER 1 (Low-Pass 24 dB) ] ---+\n                                                         +===&gt; [ EFFECTS / OUTPUT ]\n[ SAMPLE NOISE  ] ===&gt; [ FILTER 2 (High-Pass Cut)  ] ---+\n\nSERIAL FILTERING:\n[ OSC 1 ] ===&gt; [ FILTER 1 ] ===(F1 to F2 Toggle: ON)===&gt; [ FILTER 2 ] ===&gt; [ OUTPUT ]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["Clicking the <strong><code>1</code></strong> and <strong><code>2</code></strong> toggles on any oscillator header dictates which filter processes that specific generator.","Activating the <strong><code>Filter 1 to Filter 2</code></strong> switch routes the entire output of Filter 1 directly through Filter 2, enabling multi-stage serial sculpting."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 4.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-3-4","title":"10.3.4 — The Vital Unison Engine: Up to 16 Voices","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.4 — The Vital Unison Engine: Up to 16 Voices","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Every oscillator in Vital can expand from a single voice up to <strong>16 simultaneous detuned unison voices</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ SINGLE OSCILLATOR ] ===(Set Unison = 16 Voices)===&gt; [ 16 INDEPENDENT DETUNED SAWTOOTHS ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                             VITAL UNISON CONTROLS                             |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Voices ($1 - 16$)**| Dictates the number of stacked voices per note.        |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Detune Knob**      | Sets the total frequency spread in cents between outer |\n|                      | unison voices ($0\\% \\text{ to } 100\\%$).               |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Blend Slider**     | Balances the volume between the center fundamental     |\n|                      | voice and the outer detuned stereo voices.             |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Stereo Spread**    | Pans unison voices from mono center ($0\\%$) to full    |\n|                      | hard-left/hard-right stereo width ($100\\%$).           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.6 (Unison Engine)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-3-5","title":"10.3.5 — Unison Detune, Blend, and Spread Calibration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.5 — Unison Detune, Blend, and Spread Calibration","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Detune Calibration:</strong> For subtle thickness, set Detune between 5% - 12%. For massive festival supersaws, push Detune to 18% - 30%. Exceeding 40% causes severe disharmony.","<strong>Blend Calibration:</strong> Setting Blend to 100% makes all 16 voices equal in volume. Lowering Blend to 50% makes the center fundamental louder, preserving a focused pitch core while outer voices supply stereo width."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.6"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-3-6","title":"10.3.6 — The 16-Voice Euphoric Supersaw Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.6 — The 16-Voice Euphoric Supersaw Recipe","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: 16-VOICE EUPHORIC SUPERSAW                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 1**     | Waveform: **Sawtooth**, Unison: **16 Voices**          |\n|                      | Detune: **22%**, Blend: **75%**, Stereo Spread: **100%**|\n|                      | Phase: **Random 100%** (Smooth onset, zero flanging)   |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 2**     | Waveform: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **+1** (Higher Octave) |\n|                      | Unison: **12 Voices**, Detune: **16%**, Level: **-4 dB**|\n|                      | Phase: **Random 100%**                                 |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FILTER 1**         | Mode: **Analog LP24**, Cutoff: **12 kHz**, Res: **0.10**|\n|                      | Route Osc 1 and Osc 2 to **Filter 1**                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **ENVELOPE 1 (Amp)** | Attack: **8 ms**, Decay: **1.2 s**, Sustain: **0.80**,  |\n|                      | Release: **350 ms**                                    |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **VOICING PANEL**    | **Polyphony: 8 Voices**                                |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A massive, shimmering, stadium-filling 28-voice composite supersaw chord stack."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §12","<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2 &amp; 4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-3-7","title":"10.3.7 — The Voicing Panel: Polyphony and Glide","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.7 — The Voicing Panel: Polyphony and Glide","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Located in the bottom-right corner, the <strong>Voicing Panel</strong> configures master voice management:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| Voicing Control  | Range / Modes         | Sound Design Function                              |\n+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| **Polyphony**    | $1 - 32\\text{ Voices}$| Master polyphonic voice limit (Set to 1 for mono). |\n+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| **Mono / Poly**  | Mode Selector         | Locks synth to single-note monophonic bass/lead.   |\n+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| **Legato**       | Toggle On / Off       | Glides pitch ONLY on overlapping key strikes.      |\n+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| **Glide Time**   | $0\\text{ ms} - 5.0\\text{ s}$| Portamento pitch slide duration between notes.     |\n+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| **Glide Slope**  | Curve Slider          | Shapes pitch slide acceleration (Linear vs Exp).   |\n+------------------+-----------------------+----------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.7 (Voicing &amp; Portamento)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-3-8","title":"10.3.8 — Direct Out Toggle: Sub-Bass Isolation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.3.8 — Direct Out Toggle: Sub-Bass Isolation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In professional sound design, sub-bass frequencies (30 Hz - 80 Hz) must remain completely clean, undistorted, and monophonic."},{"type":"prose","html":"Each oscillator header in Vital features a dedicated <strong><code>Direct Out (D)</code></strong> switch:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ OSC 1 (Detuned Mid Saws) ] ===&gt; [ FILTER 1 ] ===&gt; [ CHORUS / DISTORTION / REVERB ] ===&gt; Audio Out\n                                                                                               +\n[ OSC 3 (Pure Sine Sub)    ] ===(DIRECT OUT = ON)============================================&gt; Audio Out\n                                (Bypasses Filter, Distortion, Chorus, and Reverb completely!)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Activating <strong>Direct Out</strong> on Oscillator 3 routes its pure sine wave sub-bass directly to the master output, completely bypassing all internal filters, heavy distortion, chorus, and reverbs. "},{"type":"prose","html":"This ensures your mid-range growls can be distorted aggressively while your sub-bass remains mathematically pristine and mono-compatible."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.8 (Direct Out Routing)","<em>Dubstep-Production-Beginner-Live12-Launchkey.md</em>, §6.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-10-4-4","code":"10.4","title":"Modulation Matrix & Envelopes/LFOs","blurb":"- Master Vital's real-time Drag-and-Drop Modulation Routing Architecture. - Toggle and calibrate Bipolar (±) and Unipolar (+) parameter modulation arcs.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-39","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Master Vital's real-time <strong>Drag-and-Drop Modulation Routing Architecture</strong>.","Toggle and calibrate <strong>Bipolar</strong> (±) and <strong>Unipolar</strong> (+) parameter modulation arcs.","Operate the graphical <strong>Bezier Curve Envelopes (1–3)</strong> and <strong>Custom Waveform LFOs (1–4)</strong>.","Configure LFO synchronization and trigger modes: <strong>Trigger (Retrigger)</strong>, <strong>Sync</strong>, <strong>Loop (Free)</strong>, and <strong>Envelope (One-Shot)</strong>.","Navigate the full-screen <strong>Modulation Matrix Tab</strong> to manage complex routing lists, auxiliary scaling sources, and non-linear transfer curves.","Configure Vital's <strong>4 Master Macro Knobs</strong> and implement <strong>Secondary Modulation (Modulating Modulators)</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-4-1","title":"10.4.1 — Drag-and-Drop Modulation Routing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.1 — Drag-and-Drop Modulation Routing","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital eliminates complex text-menu matrix routing via direct visual assignment:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ MODULATOR HEADER (e.g. LFO 1) ] ===&gt; [ CLICK &amp; DRAG CROSSHAIR ICON (+) ]\n                                                        |\n                                                        v\n                                       [ HOVER OVER ANY TARGET KNOB ]\n                                       (Filter Cutoff, Wavetable Pos, Warp, Drive)\n                                                        |\n                                                        v\n                                       [ RELEASE MOUSE TO ASSIGN ]\n                                                        |\n                                                        v\n                                       [ DRAG CIRCULAR RADIAL ARC TO SET DEPTH ]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["A bright circular <strong>Modulation Arc</strong> illuminates around the target knob indicating the exact travel boundaries of the modulation signal in real time."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 5 (Modulation System)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-4-2","title":"10.4.2 — Bipolar vs. Unipolar Arcs","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.2 — Bipolar vs. Unipolar Arcs","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                      VITAL MODULATION POLARITY MODES                          |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Unipolar ($+$)**   | The modulation travels **only clockwise (positive)**   |\n| (Single Sided)       | from the knob's resting position. Ideal for Filter     |\n|                      | Cutoff sweeps starting from a low base floor.          |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Bipolar ($\\pm$)**  | The modulation swings **symmetrically above and below**|\n| (Dual Sided)         | the knob's center resting position. Ideal for Pitch    |\n|                      | Vibrato and Stereo Auto-Pan.                           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>How to Toggle:</strong> Double-click (or right-click) directly on the circular modulation arc around any knob to instantly switch between <strong>Unipolar</strong> and <strong>Bipolar</strong> mode."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 5.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-4-3","title":"10.4.3 — Vital Envelopes: Graphical Bezier Curves","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.3 — Vital Envelopes: Graphical Bezier Curves","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital provides three dedicated, high-resolution graphical envelopes:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ ENVELOPE 1 ] ---&gt; Hardwired directly to master Amp volume (VCA).\n[ ENVELOPE 2 ] ---&gt; Assignable freely to Filter Cutoff, Wavetable Position, or Pitch.\n[ ENVELOPE 3 ] ---&gt; Auxiliary modulation envelope for transient clicks and effects.</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Bezier Point Dragging:</strong> Every segment (Attack, Decay, Release) features a central circular control point. Dragging this point shapes the curve tension continuously from steep exponential punches to gentle logarithmic swells."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 5.2 (Envelope Generators)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-4-4","title":"10.4.4 — Vital LFOs: Custom Vector MSEG Editor","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.4 — Vital LFOs: Custom Vector MSEG Editor","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital features four independent LFOs (<strong>LFO 1 – 4</strong>) that operate as fully featured Multi-Segment Envelope Generators (MSEGs):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                          VITAL LFO CANVAS TOOLS                               |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Double-Click**     | Adds a new vector curve node anywhere on the timeline. |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Right-Click Node** | Deletes an existing node or changes its curve type.    |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Shift + Drag**     | Snaps nodes to the horizontal rhythmic grid.           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Paint Brush Mode** | Draws stepped 16th-note rhythmic patterns instantly.   |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"You can draw complex multi-peak riddim wobble curves, rhythmic step sequences, or organic random drift patterns in seconds."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 5.3 (LFO Editor &amp; Tools)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-4-5","title":"10.4.5 — LFO Playback and Trigger Modes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.5 — LFO Playback and Trigger Modes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| LFO Mode         | Operational Timing Behavior                                        |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Trigger**      | Retriggers phase to $0^\\circ$ on every note-on; tracks grid sync.   |\n|                  | (Standard mode for rhythmic bass wobbles and plucks).              |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Sync**         | Continuously locks phase to the DAW project transport timeline.    |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Loop (Free)**  | Free-running un-synced periodic oscillation in $\\text{Hz}$.        |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Envelope**     | Plays through the custom drawn curve ONCE and halts (One-Shot MSEG)|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 5.4 (LFO Trigger Modes)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-4-6","title":"10.4.6 — The Matrix Tab: Master Routing Matrix","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.6 — The Matrix Tab: Master Routing Matrix","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Clicking the <strong><code>Matrix</code></strong> tab in Vital's top header opens a spreadsheet-style overview of all active modulation connections:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                      VITAL MODULATION MATRIX                                      |\n+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+\n| SOURCE            | DESTINATION       | AMOUNT            | SCALE / REMAP     | CURVE SHAPE       |\n+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+\n| **LFO 1**         | Filter 1 Cutoff   | $+0.75$           | Mod Wheel (CC 1)  | Linear ($1:1$)    |\n| **Envelope 2**    | Osc 1 Warp Pos    | $+0.60$           | Velocity          | Exponential Curve |\n| **LFO 2**         | Master Pan        | $\\pm 0.40$ (Bip)  | None              | Linear            |\n| **Macro 1**       | Distortion Drive  | $+0.85$           | None              | S-Curve Curve     |\n+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+-------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Curve Remap Column:</strong> Allows you to draw a custom non-linear response curve for that specific routing (e.g., forcing a linear mod wheel to apply an exponential curve to filter cutoff)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 6 (The Modulation Matrix)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-4-7","title":"10.4.7 — Vital Macros (1–4)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.7 — Vital Macros (1–4)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital exposes <strong>4 Master Macro Knobs</strong> in the top-left corner:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Launchkey Encoder 1 ] ===&gt; [ Vital Macro 1 (Titled: \"CUTOFF\") ] ===&gt; [ Filter 1 &amp; Filter 2 Cutoff ]\n[ Launchkey Encoder 2 ] ===&gt; [ Vital Macro 2 (Titled: \"WARP\")   ] ===&gt; [ Osc 1 &amp; Osc 2 Spectral Warp ]\n[ Launchkey Encoder 3 ] ===&gt; [ Vital Macro 3 (Titled: \"DRIVE\")  ] ===&gt; [ Distortion &amp; Comp Amount ]\n[ Launchkey Encoder 4 ] ===&gt; [ Vital Macro 4 (Titled: \"SPACE\")  ] ===&gt; [ Delay &amp; Reverb Wet Mix ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"Double-clicking a Macro title allows you to rename it. Live 12 and the Launchkey MK4 automatically detect the custom macro names in Plugin Mode."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 5.5 (Macro Mapping)","<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §9"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-4-8","title":"10.4.8 — Secondary Modulation (Modulating Modulators)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.4.8 — Secondary Modulation (Modulating Modulators)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In the <strong>Matrix Tab</strong>, you can assign an auxiliary modulation source to control the <strong>Depth Amount</strong> of an existing routing (the <strong>Scale Column</strong>):"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">Primary Routing:   [ LFO 1 ] ===(Modulates)===&gt; [ Filter 1 Cutoff ]\nScale Assignment:  [ MOD WHEEL (CC 1) ] ===(Controls Depth of LFO 1 Routing)===&gt; [ Modulation Amount ]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["When the Mod Wheel is at 0, LFO 1 has zero effect on the filter.","Pushing the physical Mod Wheel upward smoothly introduces the LFO 1 filter wobble into the sound."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 6.1 (Auxiliary Scale Routing)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-10-5-5","code":"10.5","title":"Vital Effects Rack","blurb":"- Master Vital's dedicated internal Effects Rack featuring 9 modular, re-orderable DSP processing units. - Deploy specialized Distortion Algorithms: Soft Clip, Hard Clip, Linear Fold, Sine Fold, Bitcrush, and Downsample.","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-40","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Master Vital's dedicated internal <strong>Effects Rack</strong> featuring 9 modular, re-orderable DSP processing units.","Deploy specialized <strong>Distortion Algorithms</strong>: <strong>Soft Clip</strong>, <strong>Hard Clip</strong>, <strong>Linear Fold</strong>, <strong>Sine Fold</strong>, <strong>Bitcrush</strong>, and <strong>Downsample</strong>.","Calibrate the integrated <strong>Multiband Dynamics Compressor</strong> (OTT-style simultaneous upward and downward compression).","Operate <strong>Time &amp; Modulation FX</strong>: <strong>Delay</strong> (Ping-Pong / Stereo), <strong>Reverb</strong>, <strong>Chorus</strong>, <strong>Flanger</strong>, and <strong>Phaser</strong>.","Re-order effect modules dynamically via drag-and-drop to evaluate the acoustic impact of serial processing order (e.g., <strong>Distortion → Reverb</strong> vs. <strong>Reverb → Distortion</strong>)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-5-1","title":"10.5.1 — Vital FX Rack Overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.1 — Vital FX Rack Overview","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Clicking the <strong><code>Effects</code></strong> tab in Vital's top navigation bar reveals a dedicated 9-slot studio effects rack:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                       VITAL EFFECTS RACK                                          |\n+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------------------+\n| 1. CHORUS           | 2. COMPRESSOR       | 3. DELAY            | 4. DISTORTION                   |\n| 5. EQ               | 6. FILTER FX        | 7. FLANGER          | 8. PHASER                       |\n| 9. REVERB           |                     |                     |                                 |\n+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------+---------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["Every effect module contains a dedicated <strong>Power Switch</strong>, <strong>Wet/Dry Mix Slider</strong>, and internal parameters that can be modulated directly by Vital's LFOs and Envelopes."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 7 (Effects Section Overview)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-5-2","title":"10.5.2 — Distortion in Vital: Clipping and Wave-Folding","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.2 — Distortion in Vital: Clipping and Wave-Folding","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Distortion Module</strong> provides six distinct saturation and destruction algorithms:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Distortion Model | Harmonic Transfer Function &amp; Sound Design Role                     |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Soft Clip**    | Smooth hyperbolic tangent saturation; adds warm analog density.    |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Hard Clip**    | Sharp digital threshold clipping; aggressive square-wave harmonics.|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Linear Fold**  | Folds back peaks exceeding $0\\text{ dBFS}$; classic wave-folding.   |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Sine Fold**    | Smooth trigonometric wave-folding; metallic neurofunk bass grit.   |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Bitcrush**     | Quantizes bit-depth ($1-16\\text{ bits}$); lo-fi chiptune crunch.   |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Downsample**   | Reduces digital sampling rate; introduces aggressive aliasing tones|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Filter Stage Toggle:</strong> Includes an integrated pre- or post-distortion filter with Low-Pass, Band-Pass, and High-Pass modes."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 7.1 (Distortion Processor)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-5-3","title":"10.5.3 — The Multiband Compressor (OTT Architecture)","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.3 — The Multiband Compressor (OTT Architecture)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vital integrates a world-class <strong>3-Band Multiband Dynamics Compressor</strong> based on the legendary OTT processing technique:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ INPUT SIGNAL ] ===&gt; Splits into 3 Frequency Bands (Low, Mid, High)\n                               |\n                               +---&gt; High Band: Upward + Downward Dynamics\n                               +---&gt; Mid Band:  Upward + Downward Dynamics\n                               +---&gt; Low Band:  Upward + Downward Dynamics\n                               |\n                               v\n[ GLUED, HYPER-DENSE, PUNCHY COMPOSITE OUTPUT ]</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Compressor Mode  | Dynamics Processing Topology                                       |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Single Band**  | Standard downward peak leveling compressor.                        |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Multiband**    | **Simultaneous Upward &amp; Downward Multiband Compression:**          |\n| (OTT Style)      | - Pulls quiet subtle harmonics UPward.                             |\n|                  | - Clamps harsh transient peaks DOWNward.                           |\n|                  | - Injects immense density, presence, and crunch into basslines.    |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Calibration Tip:</strong> For aggressive dubstep growls and modern supersaws, set Compressor to <strong>Multiband</strong>, pull the Mid-Band downward threshold down, and set Mix to 60%-80%."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 7.2 (Compressor Section)","<em>Dubstep-Production-Beginner-Live12-Launchkey.md</em>, §6.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-5-4","title":"10.5.4 — Stereo and Ping-Pong Delay","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.4 — Stereo and Ping-Pong Delay","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Delay Module</strong> features high-precision time-domain echo processing:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Modes:</strong> <em>Mono</em>, <em>Stereo Independent</em>, and <em>Ping-Pong</em> (bounces echoes between Left and Right speakers).","<strong>Tempo Sync:</strong> Musical metric divisions (1/4, 1/8D, 1/16T) or free time in milliseconds.","<strong>Feedback &amp; Damping:</strong> High-cut and low-cut filters inside the feedback loop prevent low-end mud from accumulating across successive repeats."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 7.3 (Delay Processor)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-5-5","title":"10.5.5 — Reverb Engine","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.5 — Reverb Engine","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The <strong>Reverb Module</strong> models high-density acoustic spaces:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Size &amp; Decay Time:</strong> Adjusts virtual room volume from tiny metallic resonant boxes (0.2 s) to massive ambient halls (10 s).","<strong>Low Cut:</strong> High-pass filter on the reverb input; <strong>always set between 150 Hz - 300 Hz on synth patches</strong> to prevent low-end rumble from washing out the mix.","<strong>Chorus Knob:</strong> Adds pitch modulation inside the reverb tail for shimmering lush pads."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 7.4 (Reverb Engine)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-5-6","title":"10.5.6 — Modulation FX: Chorus, Flanger, and Phaser","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.6 — Modulation FX: Chorus, Flanger, and Phaser","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Mod FX Module    | Acoustic Mechanism &amp; Stereo Function                               |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Chorus**       | 4-to-8 voice multi-tap delay pitch shifting for wide stereo spread.|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Flanger**      | Ultra-short comb delay ($&lt;10\\text{ ms}$) with feedback for jet sweeps|\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **Phaser**       | Multi-stage all-pass phase shifting creating sweeping notches.     |\n+------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 7.5–7.7"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-5-7","title":"10.5.7 — Post-Processing EQ and Filter FX","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.7 — Post-Processing EQ and Filter FX","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>EQ Module:</strong> 3-band parametric EQ (Low Shelf, Parametric Mid Peak, High Shelf) with graphical spectrum display for surgical post-synth resonance cleanup.","<strong>Filter FX:</strong> A secondary insert filter module inserted directly into the effects chain (useful for post-distortion low-pass smoothing)."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 7.8"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-5-8","title":"10.5.8 — Reordering Effects: The Processing Order Law","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.5.8 — Reordering Effects: The Processing Order Law","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Vital, <strong>you can click and drag any effect module vertically to alter the signal chain processing order</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">CONFIGURATION A (Clean Space):\n[ SYNTH ] ===&gt; [ DISTORTION ] ===&gt; [ COMPRESSOR ] ===&gt; [ REVERB / DELAY ] ===&gt; Audio Out\n(Distortion crunches the synth voice; Reverb adds clean, smooth space around the distorted sound)\n\nCONFIGURATION B (Industrial Destruction / Wall-of-Noise):\n[ SYNTH ] ===&gt; [ REVERB / DELAY ] ===&gt; [ DISTORTION ] ===&gt; [ COMPRESSOR ] ===&gt; Audio Out\n(Reverb tail is fed directly into the Distortion; every echo reflection is crushed into a massive, dirty wall of fuzz)</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Rule of Thumb:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["Standard EDM / House / Trance → <strong>Distortion BEFORE Reverb</strong>.","Industrial / Shoegaze / Lo-Fi → <strong>Reverb BEFORE Distortion</strong>."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 7 (Rack Reordering)","<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 9.4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"sounddesign-m-10-6-6","code":"10.6","title":"Vital Patch Lab","blurb":"- Construct eight production-grade, genre-standard patches completely from an initialized (init) state in Vital: 1. SD_Vital_ModernDubstepGrowl","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"sounddesign-l-o-41","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Learning Objectives","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Construct eight production-grade, genre-standard patches completely from an initialized (<code>init</code>) state in <strong>Vital</strong>:","<code>SD_Vital_ModernDubstepGrowl</code>","<code>SD_Vital_FestivalSupersaw</code>","<code>SD_Vital_FutureBassChords</code>","<code>SD_Vital_808TrapSubGlide</code>","<code>SD_Vital_OrganicMalletPluck</code>","<code>SD_Vital_EvolvingWavetablePad</code>","<code>SD_Vital_NeuroReeseVariant</code>","<code>SD_Vital_MetallicLaserLead</code>","Configure Vital's <strong>4 Master Macros</strong> for physical control via the Novation Launchkey MK4 in Plugin Mode.","Troubleshoot voice overload CPU spikes and phase smearing.","Complete the <strong>Gate H Self-Assessment Checkpoint</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-1","title":"10.6.1 — Patch 1: Modern Dubstep Growl","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.1 — Patch 1: Modern Dubstep Growl","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: MODERN DUBSTEP GROWL BASS                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 1**     | Table: **Basic Shapes (Frame 128 / Saw)**              |\n|                      | Spectral Warp: **Formant Scale** (Warp Knob @ 25%)     |\n|                      | Route to **Filter 1**                                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 2**     | Table: **Harmonic Series**, Octave: **0**, Level: **-8 dB**|\n|                      | Spectral Warp: **Harmonic Stretch** (Warp Knob @ 30%)  |\n|                      | Route to **Filter 1**                                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FILTER 1**         | Mode: **Dirty LP24**, Cutoff: **220 Hz**, Res: **0.40**|\n|                      | Drive: **+4.0 dB**                                     |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **MODULATION**       | **LFO 1:** Mode **Trigger**, Rate **1/4th Note Sync**   |\n|                      | - Draw guttural multi-peak Bezier curve               |\n|                      | - Route LFO 1 to **Osc 1 Formant Scale (+65%)**       |\n|                      | - Route LFO 1 to **Osc 2 Harmonic Stretch (+40%)**    |\n|                      | - Route LFO 1 to **Filter 1 Cutoff (+70%)**           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **EFFECTS RACK**     | 1. **Distortion:** Mode **Soft Clip**, Drive **+14 dB** |\n|                      | 2. **Compressor:** Mode **Multiband (OTT)**, Mix **75%**|\n|                      | 3. **Reverb:** Size **15%**, Low Cut **300 Hz**, Mix **12%**|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **VOICING PANEL**    | **Mono**, Glide: **Off**                               |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A snarling, vocal, high-energy modern dubstep growl with aggressive formant sweeping and multiband OTT crunch."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Dubstep-Production-Beginner-Live12-Launchkey.md</em>, §6.2","<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2 &amp; 7"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-2","title":"10.6.2 — Patch 2: Festival Supersaw Stack","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.2 — Patch 2: Festival Supersaw Stack","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: FESTIVAL SUPERSAW STACK                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 1**     | Waveform: **Sawtooth**, Unison: **16 Voices**, Detune: **24%**|\n|                      | Blend: **75%**, Spread: **100%**, Phase: **Random 100%**|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 2**     | Waveform: **Sawtooth**, Octave: **+1**, Unison: **12 Voices**|\n|                      | Detune: **18%**, Level: **-3 dB**, Phase: **Random 100%**|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 3**     | Waveform: **Square**, Octave: **-1**, Level: **-8 dB** |\n|                      | (Adds low-mid hollow body)                             |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FILTER 1**         | Mode: **Analog LP24**, Cutoff: **8.5 kHz**, Res: **0.10**|\n|                      | Keytracking: **100%**                                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **EFFECTS RACK**     | 1. **Compressor:** Mode **Multiband**, Mix **60%**     |\n|                      | 2. **Chorus:** Voices **4**, Mix **25%**               |\n|                      | 3. **Reverb:** Size **60%**, Decay **2.5 s**, Mix **20%**|\n|                      | 4. **Delay:** Mode **Ping-Pong**, Sync **1/8D**, Mix **15%**|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **VOICING PANEL**    | **Polyphony: 8 Voices**                                |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A wide, stadium-sized 28-voice supersaw chord stack with lush stereo chorus and ping-pong delay reflections."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2 &amp; 7"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-3","title":"10.6.3 — Patch 3: Future Bass Chords","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.3 — Patch 3: Future Bass Chords","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: FUTURE BASS CHORD STACK                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **CORE OSCILLATORS** | Configure Festival Supersaw (Patch 2 above).           |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **PITCH ONSET GESTURE**| **Envelope 2:** Attack **0 ms**, Decay **45 ms**, Sust **0.0**|\n|                      | Route Env 2 to **Osc 1 &amp; 2 Pitch (+12 st)** (Fast Up-Lift)|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **RHYTHMIC PUMP**    | **LFO 1:** Mode **Sync**, Rate **1/4th Note**, Shape **Saw Ramp**|\n|                      | Route LFO 1 to **Master Amp Level / Filter Cutoff (+80%)**|\n|                      | (Creates automated 4-on-the-floor sidechain pump)      |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> Modern future bass chord stabs featuring an expressive +1 octave transient pitch scoop and heavy sidechain pump."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Ultimate Sound Design: Mastering Sound Design</em>, Section 9.5 (Future Bass Architecture)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-4","title":"10.6.4 — Patch 4: 808 Trap Sub with Glide","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.4 — Patch 4: 808 Trap Sub with Glide","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: 808 TRAP SUB WITH GLIDE                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 1**     | Waveform: **Pure Sine**, Octave: **-2**, Level: **0.0 dB**|\n| (Sub Body)           | Phase: **0 degrees (Retrigger)**                       |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **SAMPLE OSC**       | Sample: **Analog Click**, Level: **-12 dB**, Loop: **Off**|\n| (Transient Hit)      | (Injects 10ms transient slap)                          |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **ENVELOPE 2 (Pitch)**| Attack: **0 ms**, Decay: **22 ms**, Sustain: **0.0**    |\n|                      | Route Env 2 to **Osc 1 Pitch (+24 st)**                |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **EFFECTS RACK**     | **Distortion:** Mode **Soft Clip**, Drive **+8.5 dB**  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **VOICING PANEL**    | Mode: **Mono**, **Legato Toggle: ON**, Glide Time: **110 ms**|\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A hard-hitting trap 808 that glides seamlessly across multi-octave slides during legato keyboard phrasing."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Dubstep-Production-Beginner-Live12-Launchkey.md</em>, §6.1"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-5","title":"10.6.5 — Patch 5: Organic Mallet Pluck","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.5 — Patch 5: Organic Mallet Pluck","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: ORGANIC MALLET PLUCK                                     |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 1**     | Table: **Square / Bell**, Octave: **0**                |\n|                      | AEG 1: Attack **0 ms**, Decay **320 ms**, Sustain **0.0**|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **SAMPLE OSC**       | Sample: **Wood Marimba Strike**, Keytrack **100%**, Level: **-6 dB**|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FILTER 1**         | Mode: **Comb+**, Cutoff **1.2 kHz**, Feedback **70%**  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **EFFECTS RACK**     | **Delay:** Mode **Ping-Pong**, Sync **1/8th**, Mix **20%**|\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> An acoustic-synthetic hybrid mallet pluck with tactile wooden attack and metallic comb resonance."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2 &amp; 4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-6","title":"10.6.6 — Patch 6: Evolving Wavetable Ambient Pad","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.6 — Patch 6: Evolving Wavetable Ambient Pad","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: EVOLVING 3D WAVETABLE PAD                                |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 1**     | Table: **Vocal Formants**, Unison: **4 Voices**, Detune: **10%**|\n|                      | Warp: **Vocode (30%)**                                 |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 2**     | Table: **Glass / Shimmer**, Octave: **+1**, Unison: **4 Voices**|\n|                      | Warp: **Inharmonic Smear (20%)**, Level: **-4 dB**     |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **MODULATION**       | **LFO 1:** Mode **Free Loop**, Rate **0.08 Hz** (Slow) |\n|                      | - Route LFO 1 to **Osc 1 Wavetable Pos (+75%)**        |\n|                      | - Route LFO 1 to **Osc 2 Wavetable Pos (+60%)**        |\n|                      | - Route LFO 1 to **Filter 1 Cutoff (+40%)**            |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **EFFECTS RACK**     | **Reverb:** Size **85%**, Decay **4.5 s**, Mix **35%** |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A continuously morphing, ethereal ambient soundscape that shifts through dozens of spectral frames over 12-second cycles."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2 &amp; 5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-7","title":"10.6.7 — Patch 7: Neurofunk Reese Variant","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.7 — Patch 7: Neurofunk Reese Variant","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: NEUROFUNK WARPED REESE                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 1**     | Table: **Sawtooth**, Unison: **6 Voices**, Detune: **14%**|\n|                      | Warp: **Harmonic Stretch** (Warp @ 35%)                |\n|                      | Route to **Filter 1**                                  |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 3**     | Table: **Sine Wave**, Octave: **-2**, Level: **0 dB**  |\n| (Clean Sub)          | **Direct Out Toggle: ON** (Bypasses Filter and FX)     |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FILTER 1**         | Mode: **Notch 24 dB**, Cutoff: **800 Hz**, Res: **0.60**|\n|                      | Route LFO 1 to **Filter 1 Cutoff (+60%)** (Phasing sweep)|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **EFFECTS RACK**     | 1. **Distortion:** Mode **Sine Fold**, Drive **+12 dB** |\n|                      | 2. **Compressor:** Mode **Multiband**, Mix **80%**     |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A snarling, phased neurofunk mid-bass layered over a pristine, un-filtered direct mono sub-bass."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Drum-and-Bass-Production-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §6.2"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-8","title":"10.6.8 — Patch 8: Metallic Laser Lead","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.8 — Patch 8: Metallic Laser Lead","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| PATCH SPECIFICATION: METALLIC LASER LEAD                                      |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| **OSCILLATOR 1**     | Table: **Sawtooth**, Warp: **Sync (Hard Sync)**        |\n|                      | Route **Env 2** to **Sync Warp (+80%)**, Env 2 Decay: **120 ms**|\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **FILTER 1**         | Mode: **Phase Disperser**, Cutoff: **1.5 kHz**         |\n+----------------------+--------------------------------------------------------+\n| **EFFECTS RACK**     | **Delay:** Mode **Ping-Pong**, Sync **1/16th**, Mix **25%**|\n| **VOICING PANEL**    | **Mono Legato**, Glide: **40 ms**                      |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Result:</em> A screaming, tearing laser lead driven by hard-sync phase sweeps and all-pass phase dispersion."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2 &amp; 4"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-9","title":"10.6.9 — Mapping Vital Macros to Launchkey MK4","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.9 — Mapping Vital Macros to Launchkey MK4","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Vital, rename your 4 Master Macros:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Macro 1:</strong> <code>CUTOFF</code> (Mapped to Filter 1 Cutoff)","<strong>Macro 2:</strong> <code>WARP</code> (Mapped to Osc 1 &amp; 2 Spectral Warp Depth)","<strong>Macro 3:</strong> <code>DRIVE</code> (Mapped to Distortion Drive &amp; OTT Mix)","<strong>Macro 4:</strong> <code>SPACE</code> (Mapped to Reverb &amp; Delay Wet Mix)"],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"In Ableton Live 12, select Vital and engage <strong>Plugin Mode</strong> on the Launchkey MK4. Encoders 1–4 immediately lock to these macro performance controls."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound-Design-Beginner-Knowledgebase.md</em>, §9"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-10","title":"10.6.10 — Troubleshooting: CPU Voice Overload","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.10 — Troubleshooting: CPU Voice Overload","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| DIAGNOSTIC: VITAL CAUSES CPU OVERLOAD SPIKES                                  |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| LIKELY CAUSE                       | IMMEDIATE CORRECTIVE ACTION              |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+\n| **Unison Voice Multiplication**    | Stacking 16 unison voices across 3 oscs  |\n|                                    | on a 16-voice polyphonic chord creates   |\n|                                    | $768\\text{ simultaneous voices}$!        |\n|                                    | Lower Polyphony to 6 voices or reduce    |\n|                                    | Unison count to 8 voices per oscillator. |\n+------------------------------------+------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 1.5"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-11","title":"10.6.11 — Troubleshooting: Why is My Sub-Bass Buzzing?","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.11 — Troubleshooting: Why is My Sub-Bass Buzzing?","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Direct Out Fix:</strong> If your sub-bass sounds buzzy or loses punch, you are running the sub oscillator through Vital's Distortion, Chorus, or Reverb rack. Click the <strong><code>Direct Out (D)</code></strong> switch on Oscillator 3 to bypass the effects rack and route clean, uncorrupted low-end directly to the master output."],"ordered":false},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Vital User Manual</em>, Section 2.8"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"sounddesign-l-10-6-12","title":"10.6.12 — Gate H: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"10.6.12 — Gate H: Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before advancing to <strong>Module 11 (Design Effects and Dynamic Sculpting)</strong>, verify your Vital and wavetable synthesis competencies:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                             GATE H CHECKLIST                                  |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| [ ] 1. Wavetable Frame Scanning:                                              |\n|     Can you explain in technical terms what happens when an LFO modulates the |\n|     Wavetable Position of a 256-frame table?                                  |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 2. Spectral Warping Literacy:                                             |\n|     Can you explain why Formant Scale warping generates vocal vowels at the   |\n|     generator stage before audio reaches a subtractive filter?                |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 3. 16-Voice Supersaw Execution:                                           |\n|     Can you construct a 16-voice detuned supersaw with random phase and wide  |\n|     stereo spread from an initialized Vital patch in under 60 seconds?        |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 4. Direct Out Sub Isolation:                                              |\n|     Can you demonstrate how to use the Direct Out switch to preserve a pure   |\n|     mono sine sub-bass while aggressively distorting mid-range oscillators?   |\n|                                                                               |\n| [ ] 5. Multiband OTT Dynamics:                                                |\n|     Can you calibrate Vital's Multiband Compressor to inject density into an  |\n|     aggressive dubstep growl without blowing out track headroom?              |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"If all 5 criteria are verified by ear and manual execution, <strong>Gate H is passed</strong>. You have achieved full mastery across all three core synthesis engines: Operator, Surge XT, and Vital. Proceed to <strong>Module 11: Design Effects and Dynamic Sculpting</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Sources:</strong>"},{"type":"list","items":["<em>Sound Design Foundations Syllabus</em>, §3 (Gate H Definition)"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
